theory.intro:
The **trapezoidal rule** estimates the area of a shape with a curved boundary by approximating the region as one or more **trapeziums**.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (3.5,0);
\coordinate (C) at (3.5,3.2);
\coordinate (D) at (0,2.0);
\draw[thick] (A) -- (B) node[midway, below] {$h$};
\draw[thick, dashed] (A) -- (D) node[midway, left] {$d_f$};
\draw[thick, dashed] (B) -- (C) node[midway, right] {$d_l$};
\draw[thick] plot [smooth] coordinates {(D) (1.2,2.8) (2.4,3.3) (C)};
\draw (A) rectangle ++(0.3,0.3);
\draw (B) rectangle ++(-0.3,0.3);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.question_text:
Use one application of the trapezoidal rule to estimate the area of this section of land.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (2.0,0);
\coordinate (C) at (2.0,4.4);
\coordinate (D) at (0,2.8);
\draw[thick] (A) -- (B) node[midway, below] {$10\text{ m}$};
\draw[thick, dashed] (A) -- (D) node[midway, left] {$14\text{ m}$};
\draw[thick, dashed] (B) -- (C) node[midway, right] {$22\text{ m}$};
\draw[thick] plot [smooth] coordinates {(D) (0.7,3.4) (1.4,4.0) (C)};
\draw (A) rectangle ++(0.25,0.25);
\draw (B) rectangle ++(-0.25,0.25);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Estimate the area of this field with a curved boundary using one application of the trapezoidal rule.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (3.6,0);
\coordinate (C) at (3.6,6.2);
\coordinate (D) at (0,5.0);
\draw[thick] (A) -- (B) node[midway, below] {$18\text{ m}$};
\draw[thick, dashed] (A) -- (D) node[midway, left] {$25\text{ m}$};
\draw[thick, dashed] (B) -- (C) node[midway, right] {$31\text{ m}$};
\draw[thick] plot [smooth] coordinates {(D) (1.2,5.8) (2.4,6.4) (C)};
\draw (A) rectangle ++(0.3,0.3);
\draw (B) rectangle ++(-0.3,0.3);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Estimate the area of this garden bed using two applications of the trapezoidal rule.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (M) at (2.0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (4.0,0);
\coordinate (D) at (0,3.0);
\coordinate (E) at (2.0,4.25);
\coordinate (C) at (4.0,4.75);
\draw[thick] (A) -- (B);
\draw[thick, dashed] (A) -- (D) node[midway, left] {$12\text{ m}$};
\draw[thick, dashed] (M) -- (E) node[midway, left] {$17\text{ m}$};
\draw[thick, dashed] (B) -- (C) node[midway, right] {$19\text{ m}$};
\draw[thick] plot [smooth] coordinates {(D) (1.0,3.8) (E) (3.0,4.6) (C)};
\node[below] at (1.0,0) {$8\text{ m}$};
\node[below] at (3.0,0) {$8\text{ m}$};
\draw (A) rectangle ++(0.3,0.3);
\draw (M) rectangle ++(0.3,0.3);
\draw (B) rectangle ++(-0.3,0.3);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The diagram shows a curved river boundary bulging outward away from the baseline.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (5,0);
\coordinate (C) at (5,2);
\coordinate (D) at (0,2);
\draw[thick] (A) -- (B) node[midway, below] {$h$};
\draw[dashed] (A) -- (D) node[midway, left] {$d_f$};
\draw[dashed] (B) -- (C) node[midway, right] {$d_l$};
\draw[thick] plot [smooth] coordinates {(D) (2.5,3.2) (C)};
\draw[dotted, thick] (D) -- (C);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Does one application of the trapezoidal rule overestimate or underestimate the true area? Explain why.

